Aye, I know her well enough, mo chara, though she’d sooner bite my tongue than hear that old name spoken. Sure, she’s just a sharp-witted widow who’s kept her cottage past the fairy fort for near forty years, and a steady hand with yarrow and willow bark when a mother’s milk runs thin. I’ll not credit her to the fairies, for she’s flesh and blood like the rest of us, just a bit quieter in her ways and clever with a poultice.
